  "title": "The minor triad",
  "summary": "Lower the 3rd by a half step and the chord turns dark.",
  "minutes": 5,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Minor triad",
      "body": "A **minor triad** is a root, a minor 3rd (3 half steps) above it, and a perfect 5th above the root. A minor is A C E. Compared with major, only the 3rd moves: one half step lower.\n\n**Why it matters:** Minor triads are the other half of most songs. Many pop songs use just four chords, and one or two of them are minor.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        57,
        60,
        64
      ],
      "labels": {
        "57": "A (root)",
        "60": "C (3rd)",
        "64": "E (5th)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "C major and C minor",
      "body": "Only the 3rd changes: E in C major, E♭ in C minor.",
